"""
## Purpose and audience
Purpose: Prove from the SIMPLE side that the `rt_black_box` optimization-barrier
extern declared by `src/lib/common/crypto/constant_time.spl` is actually backed
by the runtime, rather than silently returning nil.
Audience: compiler, runtime and crypto maintainers.

## Why this spec exists
`constant_time.spl` wraps the extern as:

    pub fn black_box(value: i64) -> i64?:
        rt_black_box(value) ?? value

The `?? value` fallback means an UNBACKED `rt_black_box` degrades `black_box`
into the identity function with no error at all — and the constant-time
guarantee (the whole reason the barrier exists) is silently lost. A test that
calls `black_box(5)` and asserts `5` therefore passes in BOTH the working and
the broken case and is worthless as an oracle.

This spec deliberately re-declares the extern itself so the raw optional is
observable BEFORE `??` erases it. See the defect class record:
doc/08_tracking/bug/unregistered_extern_silent_nil_2026-08-01.md

## Calibration: what an UNBACKED extern actually does (measured 2026-08-30)
These assertions only mean something if we know how a missing runtime symbol
behaves. Measured directly, by compiling a throwaway spec declaring
`extern fn rt_no_such_runtime_symbol_zzq_probe(value: i64) -> i64?` and calling
it, the interpreter lane FAILS CLOSED:

    semantic: unknown extern function: rt_no_such_runtime_symbol_zzq_probe
    Results: 2 total, 0 passed, 2 failed        (exit 1)

So on this lane an unbacked extern is a hard error, not a silent nil, and every
scenario below would go red rather than quietly green if `rt_black_box` lost its
backing. The calibration spec is deliberately NOT committed: it can only ever be
red, and a permanently-red file in test/ teaches the next reader to ignore red.

Note the backing that was found is the INTERPRETER extern table
(`src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/mod.rs`, which registers
`rt_black_box`). A repo-wide search found NO definition of `rt_black_box` in
`src/runtime/**` or `src/compiler_rust/runtime/**` — so this spec's green
verdict is evidence for the interpreter lane only, and says nothing about a
natively-compiled binary. That gap cannot be closed from Simple alone.

## Compatibility and limitations
Platform-neutral: no filesystem, clock or OS surface is touched.
This file is kept SEPARATE from the constant-time logic matrix on purpose: an
unregistered extern aborts compilation of the whole compilation unit (see the
calibration above), and that must not take the logic tests down with it.
"""

use std.spec

# The runtime's optimization barrier, declared here EXACTLY as
# src/lib/common/crypto/constant_time.spl declares it, but WITHOUT the
# `?? value` fallback that hides a nil return.
extern fn rt_black_box(value: i64) -> i64?

fn check(condition: bool):
    expect(condition).to_equal(true)

describe "rt_black_box runtime backing (Simple side)":
    # DISCRIMINATING. This is the assertion the whole file exists for.
    # An unbacked extern yields nil and this fails.
    it "rt_black_box returns a non-nil value":
        val observed = rt_black_box(1234567)
        check(observed != nil)

    # DISCRIMINATING. nil -> the `?? -987654321` sentinel -> mismatch.
    # The sentinel is deliberately a value the barrier could never produce
    # from the input, so a nil cannot masquerade as a correct answer.
    it "rt_black_box is the identity on a distinctive positive value":
        val observed = rt_black_box(1234567) ?? -987654321
        expect(observed).to_equal(1234567)

    # DISCRIMINATING. Zero is the classic nil-decays-to-zero value, so it is
    # checked through a sentinel that is NOT zero.
    it "rt_black_box preserves zero rather than confusing it with nil":
        val observed = rt_black_box(0) ?? 424242
        expect(observed).to_equal(0)

    # DISCRIMINATING. Exercises the negative half of the i64 range; a runtime
    # that truncates or unsigned-boxes the value fails here even when the
    # positive cases pass.
    it "rt_black_box preserves a negative value":
        val observed = rt_black_box(0 - 98765) ?? 1
        expect(observed).to_equal(0 - 98765)

    # DISCRIMINATING. A large magnitude catches i32 truncation in the boxing
    # path, which a small-value-only test cannot see.
    it "rt_black_box preserves a value beyond 32 bits":
        val big = 4294967296 + 12345
        val observed = rt_black_box(big) ?? 0
        expect(observed).to_equal(big)

    # DISCRIMINATING. Calling repeatedly with different arguments proves the
    # result tracks the INPUT; a stub that always returns one constant (or
    # always nil) cannot satisfy all three rows.
    it "rt_black_box tracks its argument across successive calls":
        val a = rt_black_box(11) ?? 0
        val b = rt_black_box(22) ?? 0
        val c = rt_black_box(33) ?? 0
        check(a == 11 and b == 22 and c == 33)
